DeepSeek’s new default is a price list with a benchmark sticker on top.  V4.1-Flash is open-weight, MIT, about a quarter of V4-Pro’s token price at $0.15 input / $0.60 output per million. 

On the company’s own four-pack it leads DeepSWE, CyberGym, and Automation-Bench, and it gets beaten badly on Terminal-Bench 3.0 by Claude Opus 5 (43 vs 30). An AA Intelligence Index of 40 is the sentence that belongs next to “edges out Opus and GPT.” It does not. 

It competes in the cheap-agent bucket with GLM-5.3-Flash.That is still enough to matter. Most tokens in the world will not be frontier tokens. 

If Flash is “good enough” for coding agents and ops glue, U.S. labs either cut price or concede the middle of the funnel. Margins that assumed $3–$15 per million do not love $0.15. Distillation allegations from Anthropic’s threat report sit in the same week; they are a legal and security story, not a license to copy anyone’s training recipe here.

What this is not: a proof that U.S. models are finished, a short ticket on NVDA, or a reason to ignore Wednesday’s FOMC (80–87% chance of +25 bp). 

Efficiency at the low end can raise GPU hours if usage explodes, or it can cap what enterprises will pay for a logo. Both can be true in different quarters. 

Read third-party evals when they land. Until then, the chart is DeepSeek’s ad. The price is the product.

